@member fair. I think the study has two important shortcomings.
1. if I understood things correctly this study uses SVM to generate multivariate evoked response patterns, and then studies variability across these patterns to understand variability across individuals. This is a problem because all multivariate methods produced biased response patterns. The weights you get from an SVM (or pcr, or any other multivariate method) do not converge on the true underlying generative process. OLS does so, but we don't have the ability to fit OLS here due to the large number of voxels relative to the small number of observations. Machine learning doesn't free us of this limitation, it just gives us a different tangential set of questions we can ask, but inferring on the topography of multivariate brain wide evoked responses is not a tangential question! It's using SVM to ask the traditional GLM question (ie questions concerned with localization of function in a population). There is a very clear example of where you overextend yourself with this, using SVM to support GLM-like reasoning, and that's where you guys write in the discussion that "our results suggest that the visual cortex is also an important contributor to pain prediction consistently across people." This is a very awkward point to highlight given that it basically seems to imply that visual cortex is implicated in pain physiology when in fact that is both a priori improbable (no invasive recordings support it), and the evidence is insufficient to substantiate it because you're relying on the pattern weights of a biased model. SVM is especially problematic because its bias is quite opaque (Bayesian methods are best because the bias is formally specified, but even pcr has a well characterized bias, however esoteric it may be, if interested I can give you the citation). This kind of reasoning which uses an SVM model as a model of neurophysiology, lies at the base of the entire paper I think. That makes it extremely hard for me to make heads or tails of what your findings mean because I can't separate signal from algorithmic bias in the results. I don't mean to attack you in particular for this because it's a systematic error people have made and continue to make in the field, but that doesn't excuse it either. The bias-variance trade off is covered in every introductory treatment of machine learning, and interpreting ML model weights as a source of insight into underlying neurophysiology should immediately be recognized as a mistake to all practitioners of these methods. If I had been a reviewer on this article, and I've understood what you did correctly, I'd have asked that it all be redone on univariate GLM maps. In principle the use of SVM seems unnecessary because both the univariate variability analysis and the multivariate RDM analysis could have been conducted on the back of a univariate GLM analysis.
2. There's no behavioral correlate to any of this variability, so how do we know any of it matters? This is a major limitation of this study because there are multiple sources of fine scale topographic variability between conspecifics that we know are of no practical relevance. For instance, we know there is stochastic variability in vascularization of the brain across individuals, and we also know that the BOLD SNR of draining veins is larger than microvascularized neural tissue, but differences in vascular topography are neither behaviorally nor clinically relevant. This means it's both likely that variability in evoked responses (whether biased or unbiased) are picking up differences in the supporting tissue (because it has better SNR), not the neural tissue, and that these differences are not functionally relevant. We don't have to appeal to such uncharitable interpretations though. We also know that during development the brain self organizes in a stochastic manner, but that the idiosyncratic topographic differences which result are (to a first approximation) trivial. Ocular dominance columns are a classic example. Here there is mosaicism in neural tissue within individuals that is heterogeneous across individuals. The particular mapping in each individual is determined by the combination of input from the eyes (which we know based on what happens when one eye is masked during the critical period) and the effects of local inhibitory signals which result in bifurcation in cortical column sensitivity such that it prefers one eye or the other and is different from neighboring columns (or at least that's what the computational models suggest). These kinds of mechanisms ensure interindividual variability but at the same time have no known physiological consequence nor any clinical relevance. In short, it's an example of functional topographic differences that have no physiological significance whatsoever. So how do we know differences between individuals in pain topography at this scale matter?

