@member I think you're taking the results further than is justified. There has always been some question regarding localization of function as a counterpoint to distributed functional perspectives, but these perspectives are both a matter of degree, and neither stands or falls based on whether or not subcortex and cerebellum are involved. I think everybody who has taken any time whatsoever to think about the problem realizes that no single brain region acts entirely in isolation, and the difference between localized and distributed function  are a less a question of absolute localization in the brain and more a question of localization with a particular kind of brain structure (e.g. the cortex).

Cortico-thalamic loops are a fundamental computational motif in the brain and suggest that thalamic and cortical function should be tightly integrated. The cerebellum likewise appears to be tightly integrated with thalamic and cortical function in turn. It's mainly studied in the context of motor control sure, but I think it's also known that the cerebellum is likely involved in a more ubiquitous albeit less understood manner across other task conditions. Likewise cortico-basal gaglia loops seem to be tightly interrelated. I don't know about the areas posterior to the central sulcus, but it seems like pretty much anywhere anterior to it there's some kind of projection to the BG. This tight interrelationship between all these structures across tasks has all been widely reported previously, and its ubiquity suggests there's some fundamental constraint which requires these different computational substrates to coordinate their function in order for any single one of them to do anything at all.

I've always interpreted this as the implicit context in which claims of functional localization are made. My understanding of the above is pretty vague, but consider a classic example: Broca's area. We say that we can localize speech production to this area, but I think there's always an implicit understanding that there's other supporting tissue involved as well. For instance nobody ever suggests that speech production is localized to broca's area to the exclusion of lips, mouth and tongue. I don't think there's any suggestion that localization to broca's area necessarily excludes subcortex and cerebellum either. Consider how alcohol impairs speech production and produces slurred speech via its effects on cerebellar function. Despite this dependence of speech production on cerebellar function though we still say that speech production is localized to broca's area. This is just one example, but I think a very illuminating one: the localist perspective even in it's most classic form is always able to recognize the importance of some kind of distributed processing, and citing the involvement of cerebellum and subcortex as a counterpoint to functional localization is a bit of a straw man.

