@member Fields "motivation-decision" model is taking a top down approach to this. He equates this to the model Vania and I were working with, but that's a false equivalence. There are really two competing explanations here, one cognitive and the other sensory. Unlike the motivation-decision model the version Vania and I advanced placed offset analgesia into the context of  early sensory processing (feature detection) in vision and audition, things like contrast enhancement by retinal ganglion cells.

The evidence for supraspinal involvement is equivocal. There has indeed been support for involvement of the PAG, and Field's outlines some of that evidence. However, he conveniently neglects to mention that there's a double dissociation between opiodergic signaling and offset analgesia, which throws a giant wrench in his theory about the involvement of "The classical descending opioid activated pain modulatory circuit." Additionally, Vania and I had an experiment specifically designed to prove a supraspinal mechanism, but it gave us a null finding: temperature changes on one arm don't induce offset analgesia in the other, but changes in temperature on the same arm affect one another. This is consistent with an intersegmental mechanism, but not a supraspinal mechanism. In other words, there are some correlations between the supraspinal circuit activity and offset analgesia, but attempts to show a causal connection have thus far turned up empty. This pushes me towards the sensory model over the cognitive model of offset analgesia.

That said, the PAG doesn't only operate through opiodoergic circuits, but it's important to consider the likelihood of a PAG mediated mechanism under the circumstances. Many people interested in offset analgesia seem to be interested in it primarily as a model for psychological modulation of pain intensity (this was the case for Vania and I, and seems to be the case for Fields too), but if the sensory model is the better model then offset analgesia is useless for studying psychological mechanisms.

