@member this addresses how you can't just use the cv folds to estimate variance of your CV estimator. Here is a google Doc where Tor and I have discussed this issue further:
https://docs.google.com/document/d/1MfdkMyStJ_fJcJSB5XKktUpma5eNGrrBlbW1B1PqI3s/edit?usp=sharing

This is what fundamentally prevents proper inference when using ML methods. It's a problem I try to solve by applying an ML method to multiple totally independent datasets (e.g. from the canlab single trials repo) and trying to use each instance of the ML application as an "observation" to generalize from, but then your n <= 15 which is not great. It's like performing a t-test with 15 observations. It's valid though (sort of, technically the datasets are drawn from different probability distributions, but it's as close as anyone gets without having a truly massive dataset available to subdivide for independent model trainings). The single trials walkthrough on canlab.github.io (http://canlab.github.io) demonstrates how this is done to establish that zscoring single trials improves model predictive performance relative to raw data or other scaling procedures. I think this lack of a variance estimator for model performance is one of the big issues limiting rigorous application of ML methods in neuroimaging. You can estimate how well your model will perform in novel data, but you can't put error bounds on that estimate.

